+---
+title: Drum Synth
+---
+
+The drum synth is an oscillator type for synthesised drums. It is a port of the drum voices from the open source LXR-02 firmware by Erica Synths, adapted to run inside a Deluge sound. The Deluge's own envelopes, filters, FX, mod matrix, automation and parameter locks all work on top of it.
+
+It has five engines. The first three are ported from the LXR-02:
+
+- **Kick / tom**: a main oscillator with a pitch envelope, plus a mod oscillator that either frequency modulates it or is mixed with it.
+- **Snare**: a pitched oscillator with a pitch envelope, plus sample-and-hold noise through its own low, high or band pass filter.
+- **Cymbal / hat**: a main oscillator frequency modulated by two more oscillators, one of them usually noise.
+
+The other two, **Drom1** and **Drom2**, come from the XIA engine set made for the Elektron Model:Cycles. See [Drom1 and Drom2](#drom1-and-drom2) below.
+
+Every engine also has a **transient**: a pitch "snap", a start-phase "offset" click, or one of twelve short click samples.
+
+## Making a drum synth kit
+
+1. In clip view on an empty clip, press **SHIFT + KIT**.
+2. The **New kit** menu opens. Turn **SELECT** to **Drum synth** and press **SELECT** (or **KIT**). Choose **Kit** for a normal, sample based kit.
+3. You get seven rows, from the bottom: **KICK**, **SNARE**, **CH** (closed hat), **OH** (open hat), **LTOM**, **HTOM** and **CYM**.
+
+The two hat rows are in choke mode, so an open hat is cut off by the next closed hat.
+
+You can also turn any row of any kit, or any synth, into a drum synth: open the sound editor, go to **OSC1 > TYPE** and choose **Drum synth**, then press **SELECT** for the pad editor. That way sampled and synthesised drums can share a kit.
+
+## Pad editor
+
+On a drum synth row, open the sound menu and choose **Drum synth** (its first item), or press **SELECT** on **OSC1 TYPE**. The pad editor opens: every pad column is one setting, shown as a level meter from the bottom row (lowest) to the top row (highest).
+
+- **Tap a pad** to set that column to that level. The screen shows the setting's name and value.
+- **Turn SELECT** to fine tune the column you last touched.
+- **Turn the horizontal encoder** to move between columns without changing anything.
+- **Audition pads** still play the row, so you can hold one and tap settings while you listen.
+- **SELECT press** opens the full drum synth menu below, with Engine and Load preset.
+- **BACK** goes up to the normal sound menu. The pad editor is its first item, **Drum synth**.
+- **SHIFT** shows the usual grid shortcuts while held.
+
+Single-choice settings (waves, osc mode, noise filter) light one pad per option instead of a meter. The transient column runs from Snap at the bottom, through Offset, to Click 12 at the top.
+
+Columns are coloured by section: white for the main osc, orange for the pitch envelope, light blue for the mod osc and FM, dark blue for the second mod osc, lime for the snare noise, pink for the transient, and green for the Deluge's own controls.
+
+| Column | Kick / tom | Snare | Cymbal / hat |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 1 | Osc wave | Osc wave | Osc wave |
+| 2 | Pitch amount | Pitch amount | FM amount |
+| 3 | Pitch decay | Pitch decay | Mod wave |
+| 4 | Pitch curve | Pitch curve | Mod pitch |
+| 5 | Osc mode | Noise mix | Mod fine |
+| 6 | FM amount | Noise rate | FM 2 amount |
+| 7 | Mod wave | Noise filter | Mod 2 wave |
+| 8 | Mod pitch | Noise cutoff | Mod 2 pitch |
+| 9 | Mod fine | Noise resonance | Mod 2 fine |
+| 10 | Transient | Transient | Transient |
+| 11 | Transient level | Transient level | Transient level |
+| 12 | Transient speed | Transient speed | Transient speed |
+| 13 | Tune (osc 1 transpose, -24 to +24 semitones across the pads) | same | same |
+| 14 | Amp decay | same | same |
+| 15 | LPF cutoff | same | same |
+| 16 | HPF cutoff | same | same |
+
+## Drum synth menu
+
+The same settings as a list: press **SELECT** in the pad editor, or go to **OSC1 > TYPE** and press **SELECT**, then **SELECT** again. Settings that the current engine doesn't use are hidden.
+
+| Setting | Engines | What it does |
+|---|---|---|
+| Engine | all | Kick / tom, Snare or Cymbal / hat |
+| Load preset | all | Replaces the drum synth settings with a starting point. Leaves the Deluge's envelopes, filters and FX alone |
+| Osc wave | all | Main oscillator wave: sine, triangle, saw, square or noise |
+| Pitch amount | kick, snare | Depth of the pitch envelope, up to 32 times the base pitch |
+| Pitch decay | kick, snare | How fast the pitch falls |
+| Pitch curve | kick, snare | Shape of the fall. 64 is linear, lower falls away faster |
+| Osc mode | kick | FM: the mod oscillator modulates the main one. Mix: the two are mixed |
+| FM amount | kick, cymbal | FM depth, or mod oscillator level in mix mode |
+| Mod wave, Mod pitch, Mod fine | kick, cymbal | The mod oscillator. Pitch is in semitones above the main oscillator, 64 = unison |
+| FM 2 amount, Mod 2 wave, Mod 2 pitch, Mod 2 fine | cymbal | The second mod oscillator |
+| Noise mix | snare | Noise level against the oscillator |
+| Noise rate | snare | Sample-and-hold rate of the noise. 127 is plain white noise |
+| Noise filter, Noise cutoff, Noise resonance | snare | The snare's own noise filter |
+| Transient | all | Snap, Offset, or Click 1 to 12 |
+| Transient level, Transient speed | all | Level and playback speed of the transient |
+
+Values run 0 to 127, as on the original hardware.
+
+The pitch of a row is set with the usual oscillator transpose and the Deluge's pitch controls, so the drum synth responds to note, pitch automation and the mod matrix like any other oscillator.
+
+## Drom1 and Drom2
+
+Drom1 and Drom2 are ported from the compiled Model:Cycles firmware they were written for. At the Cycles' own sample rate and block size they produce the original's output sample for sample, which the unit tests check.
+
+They work like any other Deluge sound engine: the envelopes, filters, FX, mod matrix and automation all apply on top.
+
+### Putting one on a row
+
+On any kit row (or synth), open the **SYNTH MODE** shortcut and choose **Drom1** or **Drom2**. On a kit row the first option reads **Sample**: choose it to go back to the row's sample. **Drum synth** gives the LXR engines.
+
+A row that wasn't a drum synth gets a percussive Deluge envelope (attack 0, decay 30, sustain 0, release 30), so each hit decays, and keeps decaying if you let go early, plus envelope 2 as a short blip for patching to pitch. Shape it with the Deluge's envelopes as usual.
+
+**OSC1 TYPE** on a drum synth row picks the variant: **L**, **B** or **C** for Drom1, **G**, **I** or **H** for Drom2, or the LXR voice.
+
+- **Drom1**: three sine operators and a carrier. Each operator's **Blendex** moves it from being heard (0) to modulating (127). L: all three modulate the carrier. B: a chain, 1 into 2 into 3 into the carrier. C: a loop, 1 into 2 into 3 and back into 1, with no carrier.
+- **Drom2**: inharmonic partials. G adds six, I adds six with a falling pitch at the start, H chains eight sines.
+
+### Controls and shortcuts
+
+The engines' settings are Deluge parameters, so they can be parameter locked, automated and put on gold knobs. They run 0-127 like the Cycles' knobs.
+
+Read the shortcut grid as an FM synth. The OSC1 column is the engine, and the OSC2, MOD1 and MOD2 columns are Drom1's three operators:
+
+| Pad | Drom1 | Drom2 |
+|---|---|---|
+| OSC1 TYPE | Variant | Variant |
+| OSC1 PULSE WIDTH | | Shape (sine > triangle > square > saw) |
+| OSC1 RETRIGGER | Punch (soft clipper) | Punch |
+| OSC1 FEEDBACK | Drive (wavefolder, saturator above 95) | Drive |
+| OSC1 WAVE INDEX | Engine decay | Engine decay |
+| OSC2 LEVEL / TRANSPOSE | Blendex 1 / Freq 1 | Alloy / Weight |
+| MOD1 LEVEL / TRANSPOSE | Blendex 2 / Freq 2 | Strike |
+| MOD2 LEVEL / TRANSPOSE | Blendex 3 / Freq 3 | |
+
+The other shortcuts work as usual: OSC1 LEVEL, TRANSPOSE and NOISE, the envelopes, filters and FX.
+
+**Custom 2 / Custom 3** (the last gold knob pair) carry the two key settings: **Blendex 1 / Freq 1** on Drom1, **Weight / Alloy** on Drom2. Hold a step and turn them to parameter lock.
+
+- **Freq 1-3**: each operator's tuning, from 24 semitones down at 0, through unison at 64, to 24 up at 127.
+- **Weight** picks Drom2's partial ratios, **Alloy** their levels (or the modulation depth on H), and **Strike** the level of a noise burst at the start of each hit.
+- **Engine decay** is the engine's own decay, on top of the Deluge's envelope. It starts at 127, which holds (about 9 seconds on Drom2), so the Deluge envelope does the shaping. Turning it down brings back the Cycles' own decay, including Drom2 H's brightness fading with it.
+
+The full list of settings, and the pad editor's columns, are in the sound menu's **Drum synth** item, or press SELECT on OSC1 TYPE.
+
+**Differences from the Cycles.**
+- The Deluge's envelope shapes the level, and the engines' own low pass is left open: use the Deluge's filters.
+- Engine decay and other once-per-block changes run about 9% slower, because the Deluge runs at 44.1 kHz rather than 48 kHz. Pitch is exact.
+- Blendex 3 runs 0 to 127 like Blendex 1 and 2. On the Cycles it sits on the fine tune slot and mirrors around 64.
+- Drom2's attack setting is split: below the middle it set the strike level, which is **Strike** here, and above it a fade-in, which is the Deluge envelope's attack.
+- The settings can be parameter locked and automated, but not yet modulated from the mod matrix.
+
+## Controls, shortcuts and gold knobs
+
+Every engine's continuous settings are Deluge parameters, so they can be parameter locked, automated, MIDI learned and put on gold knobs. They run 0-127, as on the LXR and the Cycles. The choices (waves, transient type, noise filter, osc mode) are menu settings.
+
+The shortcut grid reads as an FM synth. The **OSC1** column is the drum voice itself, and the **OSC2** and **MOD1** columns are its other parts. Pads not listed keep their usual job.
+
+| Pad | Kick / tom | Snare | Cymbal / hat |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| OSC1 TYPE | Voice: kick / tom, snare, cymbal / hat | same | same |
+| OSC1 PULSE WIDTH | Osc wave | Osc wave | Osc wave |
+| OSC1 RETRIGGER | Transient type | Transient type | Transient type |
+| OSC1 FEEDBACK | Transient level | Transient level | Transient level |
+| OSC1 WAVE INDEX | Transient speed | Transient speed | Transient speed |
+| OSC2 LEVEL | FM amount | Noise mix | FM amount |
+| OSC2 TRANSPOSE | Mod pitch | Noise rate | Mod pitch |
+| OSC2 TYPE | Mod wave | Noise filter | Mod wave |
+| OSC2 PULSE WIDTH | Mod fine | Noise cutoff | Mod fine |
+| OSC2 RETRIGGER | Osc mode (FM / mix) | | |
+| OSC2 FEEDBACK | | Noise resonance | |
+| MOD1 LEVEL | Pitch decay | Pitch decay | FM 2 amount |
+| MOD1 TRANSPOSE | Pitch amount | Pitch amount | Mod 2 pitch |
+| MOD1 TYPE | | | Mod 2 wave |
+| MOD1 PULSE WIDTH | | | Mod 2 fine |
+| MOD1 FEEDBACK | Pitch curve | Pitch curve | |
+
+Drom1 and Drom2 have their own layout: see [Drom1 and Drom2](#drom1-and-drom2).
+
+**Gold knobs.** Custom 1 tunes the drum. **Custom 2 / Custom 3** (the last pair) carry each engine's two key settings:
+
+| Engine | Custom 2 | Custom 3 |
+|---|---|---|
+| Kick / tom | Pitch amount | FM amount |
+| Snare | Noise mix | Pitch amount |
+| Cymbal / hat | FM amount | FM 2 amount |
+| Drom1 | Blendex 1 | Freq 1 |
+| Drom2 | Weight | Alloy |
+
+The other gold knobs keep their usual Deluge jobs. Hold a step and turn a knob to parameter lock it.
+
+**Snappy decay.** On any drum synth row, **Snappy decay** (in the sound menu, under **Drum synth**) makes the amp envelope's decay and release follow the square of the Deluge's usual curve: at every point it has dropped twice as many dB, so the same decay setting sounds about half as long, with a tighter body. It is off by default, saved with the row, and only changes envelope 1.
+
+**Envelope 2.** Every drum synth row starts with envelope 2 as a short blip: attack 0, decay 5, sustain 0, release 0. Patch it to pitch (or osc 1 pitch) for a transient snap.
+
+The LXR settings share parameters between engines. Switching a row from one LXR voice to another keeps each voice's settings: they are stored with the row and come back when you switch back. The settings can't be modulated from the mod matrix, because the Deluge only allows that for those parameters in FM mode.
+
+## CPU load
+
+**SHIFT + SELECT > CPU load** shows the audio engine's load while you play:
+
+- **Load**: audio render time as a percentage of real time.
+- **Peak**: the highest Load since the page was opened.
+- **Voice**: average nanoseconds per sample for one voice, filters and envelopes included.
+- **Drum**: average nanoseconds per sample for the drum synth oscillator alone.
+
+One sample lasts 22,676 ns, so a Voice reading of 900 means each playing voice uses about 4% of the CPU. Measured on hardware, a kick / tom drum synth voice costs about as much as a sample voice.
+
+## Compatibility
+
+Drum synth rows saved by earlier test builds of this drum synth load with their choices intact, but their continuous settings (pitch amount, FM amount and so on) moved onto Deluge parameters: reload a preset or set them again.
+
+
+Kits that use the drum synth won't play those rows on firmware without it.
+
+## Licence
+
+Drom1 and Drom2 are the TIA and IIA engines of the XIA engine set for the Model:Cycles, released as open source by their author and distributed here under the GPL-3.0 with the rest of the firmware.
+
+
+The drum synth code is licensed GPL-3.0-only, with the additional terms in the LXR firmware's NOTICE.md: it is marked as a modified version, and no rights to the Erica Synths or LXR trademarks are granted.
